1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,740
In this video, we'll show you how to use some of Burp's unique features for testing

2
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:14,200
with HTTP2 requests.

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,120
We'll also go over a couple of key things you need to understand about the protocol

4
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,440
in order to use it effectively.

5
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:23,280
Although Burp has supported HTTP2 for a while, we've added some major enhancements that

6
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:27,460
let you work with requests in ways that are just not possible with HTTP1.

7
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:30,900
This gives you the opportunity to explore a whole new HTTP2 attack surface which has

8
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:35,360
barely been audited due to the complete lack of any suitable tooling until now.

9
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:39,080
If you want to get an idea of the kind of attacks you can perform using these tools, check out

10
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:41,920
James Kettle's HTTP2 white paper on our research page.

11
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:45,960
I'll leave a link in the description.

12
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:50,340
Because HTTP2 is a binary protocol, the actual requests that are sent over the wire are really

13
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:52,420
a bit of a pain to work with directly.

14
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,900
So in Burp, we've made things much easier for you by displaying two different human-readable

15
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:57,900
representations of each request.

16
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:01,460
We'll look at the classic message editor view in a minute because most of the exciting

17
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:04,780
stuff is over here in the inspector panel.

18
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:08,160
First of all, under the request attributes section you can change which protocol you want

19
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:10,700
to use to send each request.

20
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:14,360
This means you can effectively upgrade and downgrade requests at will, and Burp will take

21
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,300
care of the details for you behind the scenes.

22
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:21,440
But the real power of these features becomes apparent when you open the request headers section.

23
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:24,920
Notice that I'm using HTTP1 at the moment, and the headers shown in the inspector match

24
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:27,920
up with what you can see over in the message editor.

25
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:31,360
But watch what happens when I switch to HTTP2.

26
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:35,260
Most of the headers are still basically the same, but you can now also see the pseudo headers.

27
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:38,940
In case you're not familiar with pseudo headers, these are new in HTTP2 and are used to pass

28
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:42,500
key bits of information about the request, some of which HTTP1 doesn't provide a header

29
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:44,160
for at all.

30
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:48,980
The method and path pseudo headers effectively replace the old request line, for example.

31
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:53,360
The colon prefix is just to help distinguish pseudo headers from ordinary headers.

32
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:56,780
Other than the fact that we don't show you the binary itself, this is a very accurate view

33
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:58,580
of what will be sent to the server.

34
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:02,500
Crucially, any changes you make to the headers here in the inspector won't be normalised or

35
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,660
cleaned up in any way by Burp, and you're not constrained by the limitations of any HTTP1

36
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:07,700
syntax.

37
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:12,120
This enables you to construct all kinds of advanced attacks that are exclusive to HTTP2, and currently

38
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:14,020
only possible using Burp.

39
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:16,880
We'll look at an example of this later.

40
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:20,500
Here in the message editor, notice that we still provide a representation of the request

41
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:23,560
using HTTP1 syntax.

42
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:27,540
If you make any changes here, behind the scenes Burp essentially just maps this request to

43
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:32,180
its HTTP2 equivalent, so the values of the method and path pseudo headers are derived from

44
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:34,500
the request line that you see here, for example.

45
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:38,420
Likewise, the value of the host header is used to generate the authority pseudo header, and

46
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:39,540
so on.

47
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:43,320
This is great if you're just using HTTP2 for the performance improvements it provides, and aren't

48
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:46,940
really concerned with any protocol level issues like request smuggling.

49
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:50,840
However this view is not really suitable when testing for HTTP2 specific vulnerabilities

50
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:54,540
because of the limitations imposed by the HTTP1 syntax.

51
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:58,280
For example, you won't be able to put a space in the request path here, even though this is

52
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:01,360
technically possible in the path pseudo header that this points to.

53
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:06,220
The editor also performs some lightweight normalisation to ensure that valid HTTP1 requests don't result

54
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:08,480
in invalid HTTP2 requests.

55
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:12,620
For example, you may have noticed that we automatically lowercase the header names, as this is required

56
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:14,780
by the HTTP2 spec.

57
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:18,160
We also strip the connection header and prevent you from moving the host header for the same

58
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:19,160
reason.

59
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:22,680
We've surfaced this normalisation in the editor to help you see what's happening behind

60
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:28,040
the scenes, so when you send the request you'll see this taking place.

61
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:31,720
For this reason we recommend using the inspector when you're making protocol level changes that

62
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:33,800
are specific to HTTP2.

63
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:38,160
This allows you to peel back that layer of abstraction and work more directly with the actual HTTP2 request

64
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:39,160
underneath.

65
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:43,980
As this representation is completely decoupled from HTTP1, we don't have to normalise the

66
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:47,820
changes you make here, so you can control exactly what gets sent to the server and do things

67
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:50,220
that are not possible via the editor.

68
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:54,460
So what kinds of HTTP2 specific changes can you make using these features?

69
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:57,360
Well if you've seen James' research, you'll know that one of the key differences is the

70
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:01,060
ability to inject new lines within headers, so let's start with that.

71
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:04,980
If you click on the arrow next to any header, you can drill down into its name and value.

72
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:08,740
Then to add a new line, just press the shift and return keys.

73
00:04:08,740 --> 00:04:14,320
Exposing the ability to do this is fundamental for constructing many HTTP2 specific attacks.

74
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:18,440
When I apply the changes, notice that the headers in the message editor have disappeared.

75
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:22,840
These have been replaced by a notification telling me that the request has been kettled.

76
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:26,200
This is the term we've coined in honour of our infamous director of research to describe

77
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:31,580
HTTP2 requests that can no longer be accurately represented using HTTP1 syntax, at least not without

78
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:33,120
losing information.

79
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:37,280
This doesn't necessarily mean that the request is invalid, that all depends on the server,

80
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:41,720
it just means that in its current state you can only work with it using the inspector.

81
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:44,660
In this case, I've added a new line inside a header value.

82
00:04:44,660 --> 00:04:47,340
Strictly speaking, this isn't allowed in HTTP2.

83
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:51,780
However, because these characters have no special meaning for parsers like they do in HTTP1,

84
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:56,020
it's technically possible to inject them and some servers will just tolerate this.

85
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:59,840
If we tried to write this using HTTP1 syntax, it would just look like two separate headers,

86
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:01,300
even though it's not.

87
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:03,160
This is the real power of the inspector.

88
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:08,720
You can create and send HTTP2 requests that it's just not possible to represent in HTTP1.

89
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:12,080
Using these features, you'll be able to find a number of bugs that are otherwise impossible

90
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:13,540
to test for.

91
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:16,740
Before we wrap up, I just want to point out that there are a few new repeater options that

92
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:18,980
are specific to HTTP2 handling.

93
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:21,140
You can find these on the repeater menu.

94
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:25,940
I won't go over these in detail now, but you can refer to our documentation for more information.

95
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:30,920
You can also change which protocol BURP uses by default from the project settings.

96
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:35,380
If you deselect this checkbox, BURP will use HTTP1 unless you explicitly tell it to send

97
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:38,860
an HTTP2 request using the toggle in the inspector.

98
00:05:38,860 --> 00:05:42,020
So that's a whistle-stop tour of BURP's HTTP2 features.

99
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:45,180
Most of the time, using HTTP2 will just make your attacks faster.

100
00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:48,740
But now that you can work with requests in a way that's decoupled from HTTP1 completely,

101
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:51,580
there are new possibilities for protocol-level attacks.

102
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:54,000
Just make sure you use the right protocol for the situation.

